  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=28,{x}_{2}=18$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=3.6$ and ${z}_{2}=1.1,$ respectively. Find the mean of the data set.",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{\u2015}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{\u2015}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =\\overset{\u00af}{x}+3.6\\cdot \\sigma .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =\\overset{\u00af}{x}+1.1\\cdot \\sigma .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(28-18) & =(3.6-1.1)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 10 & =2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{\u00af}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =\\overset{\u00af}{x}+3.6\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 28 & =\\overset{\u00af}{x}+3.6\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\overset{\u00af}{x} & =13.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
